hypotheses.statement:
For every rational $J\ge1$, there are effectively selectable integers $m,n\ge2$ and positive rational constants $\sigma,\delta,\eta,p,\theta<1$, with $\eta\le\sigma$, depending only on $J$, with the following property. For any finite weighted family of nonempty affine alphabets and folded bit proofs, suppose $$\varepsilon_j\le Jb_j\qquad(j=1,2,3).$$ Draw $\xi$, a standard Gaussian $c\in\mathbb R^X$, independent uniform $x\in X$ and $i\in[m]$, and all background rows independently and uniformly in $D(A_\xi)$. With probability at least $p$, the function $$b\longmapsto h_x(B[B^i_{x_{-i}}\leftarrow b],c)$$ has a Fourier coefficient of absolute value at least $\theta$ at an odd frequency of $D(A_\xi)$. Equivalently, the event is $$\label{eq:inner-atom-event}
 \max_{\alpha\in D(A_\xi)^*: \alpha(1)=1}
 \left|\mathbb E_{b\in D(A_\xi)}
 h_x(B[B^i_{x_{-i}}\leftarrow b],c)(-1)^{\alpha(b)}\right|
 \ge\theta.$$ For every folded proof, without any test assumption, one has pointwise $$\label{eq:gradient-bounds}
 \lVert h(B,c)\rVert_{2,x}\le\sigma^{-1},\qquad
 |h_x(B,c)|\le\sqrt N/\sigma.$$

For every real array $v$, $$\label{eq:box-cs}
 \mathfrak d_Y(v)^{2^m}
 \le\mathbb E_{u^0,u^1}\prod_{\omega\in\{0,1\}^m}v(u^\omega).$$ In particular the right side is nonnegative.

There is a deterministic construction, with all preliminary work depending only on $K$, of a finite rational replacement for the four tests such that $$\label{eq:uniform-finite-error}
 \lvert\operatorname{Cost}_{\mathrm{fin}}(f)-\operatorname{Cost}_\infty(f)\rvert<\frac12
 \qquad\text{for every collection of folded proof tables }f.$$ Consequently its minimum cost is less than $9/2$ in the YES case, and every proof has cost greater than $10K-1/2$ in the NO case. The rational distribution replacing $(T_0,T_1)$ has a positive integer common denominator depending only on $K$, computed by the construction.

The finite rational comparison system can be transformed deterministically into a simple undirected unweighted graph $G$ with $$\label{eq:exact-graph-optimum}
 \operatorname{OPT}_{\mathrm{uncut}}(G)
 =D_0\min_f\operatorname{Cost}_{\mathrm{fin}}(f).$$ The graph has $O_K(M_0^t)$ vertices and edges and is constructed in polynomial time, including bit complexity, for fixed $K$.

